<div style="margin:22px 0 8px; padding-bottom:3px; border-bottom:1px solid #4a4e53; font-family:Consolas,Menlo,'DejaVu Sans Mono','Courier New',monospace; font-size:12px; letter-spacing:.14em; color:#cfd2d5;">STATEMENT OF SUBJECT</div>
<div style="margin-top:10px;">I'm '''Krislyz'''! I translate and proofread Hebrew for VRChat, and I do the same for the pages on this wiki. My username is identical on almost every social platform, so if you've found it somewhere else, it's most likely me.</div>
<div style="padding-top:8px;">[https://krislyz.com/ <span style="color:#3dc9d6;">krislyz.com</span>] · [https://github.com/Krislyz <span style="color:#3dc9d6;">GitHub</span>]</div>
<div style="margin:22px 0 8px; padding-bottom:3px; border-bottom:1px solid #4a4e53; font-family:Consolas,Menlo,'DejaVu Sans Mono','Courier New',monospace; font-size:12px; letter-spacing:.14em; color:#cfd2d5;">STATEMENT OF SUBJECT (CONT.)</div>
If you've played VRChat in Hebrew, you've read my work. On VRChat's Crowdin project I've proofread 45,000+ words and translated 15,000+ myself. That makes me first in Hebrew approvals and second in Hebrew translations of all time. A translation only shows up in the app once someone approves it, and for Hebrew, more of those approvals are mine than anyone else's. Most of the text is short UI: buttons, warnings, tooltips, error messages. A four-word button looks easy until it has to be correct, natural, and worded the same way as everywhere else the app says that thing.

Consistency is where I spend most of my time. Hebrew makes you choose, in every instruction, who you're talking to: "click" and "make sure" can come out masculine, feminine, plural or as an infinitive, and a translation that switches between them from one screen to the next feels careless even when each line is fine on its own. So I check whole files for it, along with the terminology, the words that stay in English, and how names and usernames get written. Placeholders get the same treatment: they stay exactly as they are, and I move them to wherever they sound most natural in the Hebrew sentence.

<div style="margin:8px 0 12px;"><span style="color:#9aa0a6;">"click" in Hebrew, depending on who you're talking to:</span><br><span style="display:inline-block; width:13ch;">masculine</span><span lang="he" dir="rtl">לחץ</span><br><span style="display:inline-block; width:13ch;">feminine</span><span lang="he" dir="rtl">לחצי</span><br><span style="display:inline-block; width:13ch;">plural</span><span lang="he" dir="rtl">לחצו</span><br><span style="display:inline-block; width:13ch;">infinitive</span><span lang="he" dir="rtl">ללחוץ</span><br></div>

Feedback makes the work better. I listen to what other contributors say about a translation and work with Foofik, the project's other Hebrew proofreader, to get each string right. When Hebrew's right-to-left order breaks something in the app, we collect the cases and pass them to VRChat's team.
<div style="margin:22px 0 8px; padding-bottom:3px; border-bottom:1px solid #4a4e53; font-family:Consolas,Menlo,'DejaVu Sans Mono','Courier New',monospace; font-size:12px; letter-spacing:.14em; color:#cfd2d5;">ADDITIONAL BACKGROUND</div>
<div style="margin-top:10px;">Outside of translating, I love cybersecurity and exploring stuff. I like finding out how things work and what's behind them.</div>
